089de1c3 (2002 1793526578886 "guenchi" (("4gm4c2jr" . 3240) ("w5vbequy" . 14982)) (put ((kind . section) (title . "landing f16a") (src . "read --ast: a block's body as a parsed tree, references resolved\n\nA program that renders or edits store content had to parse markdown\nitself: read answers a block as data or as its own bytes, and nothing\nanswered the tree md.sc already builds. This is the first slice of the\n--ast design (spans and verbatim raw-md, the slugs index, the conflicts\nrows, --plain, --map, read by name, the rest of the README and the\noutline's rows follow); it answers the tree with each reference\nresolved to the block it names.\n\nread <id> --ast [--recursive] [--cut <cut>] answers (ok (block <id>\n<entries> (hash <h>) (ast <node> ...)) <validity> <receipt>): the\nentries are the plain read's record after its id, copied as the view\nprints them (deleted, fields, position as the pair or the conflict form,\nedges), the hash is read-version's answer (the --if-unchanged token, its\nunavailable form, or #f for a tombstone), the validity clause the plain\nread's and the receipt every read's, handed the hash already computed.\nWith --recursive the items are the block forms in read --recursive's\norder, then the versions built from the items' hashes, the listed\nvalidity and the cut. A tombstone has no tree whatever its kind; a live\nblock of a kind the markdown projection writes (project.sc's md-kinds,\nnot a list of the handler's own) has the tree of its src, front and\nheading-src being fields, bytes read as --md reads them; any other kind\nanswers (ast #f), and bytes that are not UTF-8 or a src in conflict\n(ast #f (note src-not-text)). --ast with --md, --working, --working-info\nor --signature is incompatible-ast-options, judged after the handler's\ncut and rev refusals; --writer alone changes nothing, as for a plain\nread; --cut reads the tree at the cut, references included. An admitted\nrequest is named once in the trace, a refused one not at all.\n\nThe tree is md.sc's as it stands, with one change made after parsing:\nevery (ref <lexeme> <key>) becomes (ref <lexeme> <key> <target>), the\ntarget the id of a live block or #f. reduce.sc gains the two functions,\nexported: resolve-ref-target tries two spellings in the state the read\nanswers from, the raw bracket text with its .md kept (rebuilt from the\nkey, so the trimming rule stays md.sc's), then the key, each with a\nleading ent: removed, so [[X]], [[X.md]], [[X|shown]] and [[ent:X]] all\nname a live X and a block whose id itself ends in .md (a writer's event\n805) is still reached; a tombstone never resolves. This order amends\nthe design's resolver order (its slug step comes after both, later).\nast-with-targets walks para, the inline children of em, strong and link\nafter the href, quote's blocks and each list item's blocks; code, hr and\nraw-md are leaves; md.sc's printer reads a ref by its lexeme, so the\nlonger node prints as the shorter one did and the round trip holds.\nmd.sc is unchanged. read's usage, describe sentence and flag table gain\nthe option; the README gets the grammar as md.sc emits it, the\nresolution rule, the (ast #f) cases and one sentence that the grammar\ngrows and a reader pins the shape it read.\n\nThe facade gate reads the new site: the block tag is built inside the\nitems call, so its walk sees (read . block), a tag locate also answers\nfor its spans; both name a block record, the shapes differ at the third\nelement and a reader dispatches on the verb, accepted as cross-verb\nreuse where the gate lists tags. read's run-time census invocation is\nread --recursive --ast, so the tag the static scan reads is produced;\nthe plain --recursive envelope stays pinned by read1, read-receipt and\nthe new fixture. The verb registry snapshot takes read's regenerated\nentry, and the change-stream guard's expect file lists the two scripted\nanswers that change, describe and read-usage, to be emptied by the\nfollow-up commit once this is the base.\n\nTests: test/read-ast.sc, new, 42 rows over three stores (one with a\nblock a peer moved and edited concurrently, one whose fresh writer's\nevent 805 is the block <writer>.md). Every expected tree, id, hash and\ncut is a literal bound from the setup before the read it is compared\nwith, where a row is not by design a comparison with another answer (the\nids of read --recursive, the version and the state token, the plain\nrecord, the plain validity clause, the same request in two spellings).\nRA-1 the block form whole, the entries in the record's order, the\nconflicted position, a census of one md->blocks and one parse-inline in\nthe product files; RA-2 eight spellings resolved and unresolved in a\nparagraph, a quote, a list item, em and strong, the walker on a\nconstructed link, list item and quote, and the .md id by four spellings,\nthree resolving; RA-3 code regions, the parser pinned and the --ast tree\nover them; RA-4 the recursive answer whole; RA-5 the hash against the\nversion, the versions pair and a write's state token, through set src\nand set title; RA-6 the kinds, a decision block's tree, UTF-8 and\nnon-UTF-8 bytes, a conflicted src, a tombstone single and recursive, an\nunhashable block, and a code block's record equal to the plain read's\nwith the view's fields; RA-7 the round trip; RA-8 the option refusals,\n--cut's tree and its resolution at the cut, --rev on the local route and\non a publication, the handler's order, --writer; RA-9 the plain, --md\nand --recursive envelopes pinned against literals; RA-10 one trace line\nper request; RA-11 the validity clause single and recursive. Red first\non the base with the fixture: 35 of 42 rows (the 34 --ast rows by usage,\nand the constructed-tree row with no walker to call); the base reds the\nguard's control, the registry's control and six facade-gate rows too.\nTen mutants each red: the resolver bypassed, tombstones resolved, the\npresent state for a historical read, the key alone, the validity\ndropped, a second parser, a walk skipping containers, heading-src parsed\nas body, the --md text as the answer, state-read's record copied.\nReviewed in two rounds of code review, three sends: the first send could\nnot review (a scope of a freeze carries no new file's draft, recorded)\nand went again with the fixture in the packet and the reference files as\nroots; its one finding, the code-block record unprotected, is the second\nround's row. rows-baseline takes read-ast and facade-gate's new hash.\n\nThe first full gate on the frozen tree read two fixture rows red that\nthe product's shape met for the first time, both corrected here and\ngated again. options-gate's verb-span scanner took every (cons ' inside\nthe verb table as an entry, so the inline handler's answer constructors\n(cons 'ast and (cons 'block opened spans and the option reads after\nthem were attributed to verbs the parser never had; thirteen such\nspellings sat in the table with no read after them. An entry is now a\n(cons '<verb> whose form, read as one datum from that position, passes\nfacade-gate's verb-of, a lambda as the value; one control row pins the\n46 entries that test finds, the inner error and ok spellings opening\nnone, another drives the scanner on a made-up table where a value headed\nlambda-wrapper opens no span and comments between the tokens change\nnothing. liveness compared the\nbase tree's MCP server's tools/list against both daemons byte for\nbyte, and the tool descriptions carry the daemon's describe sentences;\nthe row now follows the rule the frame row beside it already lives by,\nleft-out with its own printed line while the guard's expect file lists\ndescribe, its other five checks unchanged. Red first: the old scanner\nreds the control row and its own row on this tree, a lexical anchor\nreds the made-up table's row; the row with the expect file emptied reds\nat the same offset the gate read.\n\nGate on an isolated copy of the tree (241 fixtures): only the known\npair red (f0-ondemand COST-1 over its budget, startup-ratio C1/C3);\noptions-gate and liveness green; three python fixtures run; row\nbaseline hashes all the described versions, the line counts the ten\nnames of the previous stage with liveness's two new printed lines; no\nprocess of the run left; source alternation against the base min ratio\n1.0039 and alternation of the compiled product median 1.0012 (p10\nratios 1.0057, 1.0012, 1.0012), both within 1%. The comment-only\ncorrection after that gate leaves the fixture's reader datum equal.\n") (parent . root) (ord . 580))))
